====Adjective====
{{en-adj|more|er}} {{term-label|en|''quacker'' and ''quackest'' are rare, and probably used humorously}}

# [[falsely|Falsely]] [[present#Verb|presented]] as having [[medicinal]] [[power#Noun|powers]].
#: {{ux|en|Don't get your hopes up; that's '''quack''' medicine!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Alphabet of Scientific Gardening for the Use of Beginners|passage=In precisely the same way does a '''quack''' doctor prescribe his infallible nostrum to every patient, without taking into account differences of constitution, or [...]|year=1833|author=w:James Rennie|page=2|chapter=The Word Gardening|publisher=William Orr|location=London}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Henry D. Estabrook|title=Truth in Advertising [advertisement]|newspaper=The Duluth Herald|location=Duluth, Minn.|publisher=The Herald Company|date=5 August 1916|volume=XXXIV|issue=102|page=6|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/duluthherald8311916unse#page/n97/mode/1up/|oclc=1567044|passage=[R]ecently I examined as many newspapers and magazines as I could lay hands on just to see if I could find in them those old, alluring advertisements, ranging from the '''quack''' doctor to the '''quacker''' promoter and the '''quackest''' oracle of fate. There was nothing doing—everything as clean as a hound's tooth and as wholesome as sunshine.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Prospect before Us: Some Thoughts on the Future|location=London|publisher=[[w:Sampson Low|Sampson Low, Marston & Co.]]|year=1948|page=102|oclc=503869468|passage=Finding, perhaps, that there is no solution either in politics or in any existing religion, he [the common man] may cling to the diagnosis of the last and '''quackest''' of his doctors: he may believe that art can save himself and the world.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1976-03-27|author=F. Dudley Hart|title=History of the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis|journal=w:British Medical Journal|volume=1|issue=6012|doi=10.1136/bmj.1.6012.763|jstor=20409177|page=763|passage=When no certain cure exists, '''quack''' remedies tend to proliferate and the history of quackery and secret cures is full of extraordinary forms of treatment for the various arthritic disorders.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Journal of the Association of Food and Drug Officials|location=York, Pa.|publisher=The Association|year=1991|volume=55|page=35|issn=0898-4131|oclc=17212478|passage=They desperately want to believe something will help and for that reason they assist one another in obtaining unproven remedies. Such "helpful" promotion is generally more "'''quack'''" than fraudulent in nature.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Jenny Uglow|chapter=Allegories of Healing|title=William Hogarth: A Life and a World|location=London|publisher=w:Faber and Faber|year=1997|page=506|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Twv6XOvoBFYC&pg=PA506|isbn=978-0-571-16996-2|passage=[[w:William Hogarth|[William] Hogarth]] might have felt some sympathy for [[w:Sally Mapp|[Sally] Mapp]] as an 'irregular' expert besting pomposity, but this is topped by his sheer relish for her as the '''Quackest''' Quack of all, and female to boot. In Hogarth's print the dark goddess rules over her court of fools, men who have taken over the ancient realm of women's healing, and now profit from the people's ills and credulity.}}

